when it comes to delivering a memorable  intense experience not many games can  match up to the original Dark Souls many  fans swear by the approach from software  takes with its bleak action RPG series  has become one of the most challenging  and anxiety-inducing franchises in  recent memory with the new release of  Dark Souls remastered on switch the  series makes its way to the Nintendo  console for the first time bringing with  it its particular style of high-pressure  gameplay set within a haunting world  while it isn't as technically impressive  as other current gen releases it's still  an admirable port that reinforces what  makes Dark Souls so memorable it also  makes effective use of switches  capabilities as a portable console which  in turn offers a slightly different feel  to the souls experience that feels  surprisingly refreshing  the switch version is very much in line  with what came before with other  releases of remastered in addition to  offering up a refined online experience  the remaster features all available DLC  and along with touched up visuals  however due to the limitations of the  system's hardware it seen some clear  downgrades compared to the previous  versions of remastered while playing  through the switch version it felt more  closely to the original ps3 and 360  releases albeit far more stable running  at a solid 30 frames per second the  doctrine of Dark Souls on switch  displays at 1080p with the handheld mode  set at 720p aside from the drop in  resolution general frame rate between  docked and handheld is largely  consistent which is great when swapping  between the two different modes during a  session if you're used to Dark Souls  remasters performance on the other  platforms the switch version will likely  take some time adjusting to in addition  to some fairly short draw distances on  environmental details the audio quality  sounds far more subdued and quiet  compared to other releases while this  doesn't happen often it can create odd  moments where some sound effects get  drowned out by others  moreover playing dock mode with the joy  cones can often induce some lag with the  camera controls which can be a dire  issue during some careful platforming or  an intense combat encounter  fortunately the day one patch does work  to address these issues to some success  but other technical hiccups still linger  despite these rough edges Dark Souls on  switch is still an impressive port that  manages to keep the souls experience  intact for its new platform and the  surprisingly neat feature it's possible  to pause the game when playing in the  offline mode by backing out to the home  menu or by setting the system to sleep  if you're planning to take Dark Souls  mobile then you'll more than likely make  use of the offline mode often and the  switch version feels much more suited to  it  to properly put this version through its  paces we journey to one of the game's  most notorious levels which frustrated  and unnerved many players upon its  original release Blighttown the derelict  shanty town full of diseased creatures  was an unnerving descent into a  grotesque atmosphere filled with narrow  walkways and an infamously unstable  framerate many years later it's still  one of the game's most memorable and  feared areas and in the switch version  it's fortunately able to keep a stable  30 frames per second throughout the most  notable success that Dark Souls  remastered has on the switch aside from  the sheer fact that it runs properly on  the hardware is how it can feel much  more like an involved journey thanks to  the handheld mode you're now able to  bring Dark Souls with you on the go as a  returning player it often felt like I  was bundling up with an engrossing book  voraciously exploring and unearthing the  game's many locations in an interesting  way playing in handheld mode can make  for a more personal experience with Dark  Souls which is something that's entirely  exclusive to the switch release while  the switches portability feature can  often feel overstated for other games it  does however amplify the core of what  Dark Souls is all about over the course  of your personal story and game which is  on a road paved by defeat small  victories and occasional humiliation  you'll eventually come to a major win  making the challenging journey feel  worthwhile while Dark Souls remaster on  switch possesses some odd quirks and  isn't as technically impressive as as  current-gen counterparts it still  retains the heart of what the original  game is all about  to this day Dark Souls remains a  watershed moment for the action RPG  genre getting to re-experience many of  the game's most nerve-wracking and  iconic moments can be satisfying in its  own right but coupled with the switches  flexible play style this equally  haunting and triumphant game becomes  even more of an involved journey
 

